<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#oscan-language">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oscan language</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Oscan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Oscan was an Italic language spoken in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> by the Samnites, Campanians, Lucanians and related peoples in the second half of the first millennium BCE. It is known from several hundred inscriptions, written in a native alphabet derived from Etruscan as well as in Greek and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> letters, many from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompeii">Pompeii</a> and Capua. After the Social War it gave way to Latin.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oscan-language.jpg" alt="Oscan language" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Félix Potuit · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:OsqueBritishMuseum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Oscan is an extinct Indo-European language of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, in the Osco-Umbrian or Sabellic branch of the Italic languages, and so a close relative of Umbrian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-picene-language">South Picene</a>. It was spoken by a number of tribes, including the Samnites, the Lucani, the Aurunci and the Sidicini. Several poorly attested varieties of the eastern central Italian highlands, among them Hernican, Marrucinian and Paelignian, are conventionally grouped with it.</p><p>Oscan speakers lived in close contact with the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latium">Latium</a>, and the language had much in common with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> as well as striking differences. Oscan has p where Latin has qu, as in pis and quis, and b where Latin has v. Where Latin used volo for willing, Oscan used words from a root meaning to desire, as in herest. It is considered the most conservative of the known Italic languages.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Oscan is known from inscriptions as far back as the 5th century BCE, about 800 texts in all. The most important are the Tabula Bantina, the Oscan Tablet and the Cippus Abellanus. The Cippus Abellanus records an agreement between Abella and Nola about building near a temple of Hercules and a shared road. The Tabula Bantina sets out rules for assemblies and for magistrates' trials, with fines for those who break them. Coins inscribed in Oscan at Teanum Apulum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a> date from before 300 BCE.</p><p>It was written first in an Oscan alphabet derived from the Etruscan, which the Osci probably adopted in the 7th century BCE, though a recognisably Oscan form is attested only from the 5th. Later texts were written in a South Oscan script based on Greek and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> alphabet. In the 3rd century BCE two new signs, Í and Ú, were added to the inventory. Oscan nouns had seven cases and verbs were inflected for tense, voice, mood, person and number.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In the coastal zones of southern Italy Oscan is thought to have lived beside Greek for three centuries, from 400 to 100 BCE, in what has been called an unusual case of stable bilingualism. Over the Roman period both languages were gradually effaced. Oscan declined after the Social War, but graffiti show it remained in colloquial use. Oscan graffiti on Pompeian walls rebuilt after the earthquake of 62 CE must have been written between 62 and 79 CE. Other scholars argue that this is weak evidence for Oscan as an official language, since public inscriptions in Oscan disappeared after Roman colonisation. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> may have held political, religious and administrative importance while Oscan was regarded as a low language.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Oscan language” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the middle of the 7th century BCE the Romans adopted the Etruscan writing system, taking the three letters C, K and Q for one sound and later turning C into G; the <b>Oscan</b>, Umbrian, Faliscan and Venetic scripts also descend from Etruscan alphabets.</blockquote><a href="/empire/etruscan-league">Etruscan League · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Latin belongs to the Italic branch of the Indo-European family, which also includes <b>Oscan</b> and Umbrian, once spoken in central and southern Italy, and Faliscan, its close neighbour.</blockquote><a href="/language/latin">Latin · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 423 BCE <b>Oscan</b>-speaking Samnites seized it, and its people became known as Campani.</blockquote><a href="/state/capua">Capua</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 421 BCE Samnite-speaking Campanians captured Cumae, and it became an <b>Oscan</b> town.</blockquote><a href="/state/cumae">Cumae</a></li><li><blockquote>Ancient writers considered them of Samnite stock, and they spoke <b>Oscan</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/frentani">Frentani</a></li><li><blockquote>Their name was explained from the <b>Oscan</b> word hirpus, 'wolf', after the animal that guided their ancestors in a 'sacred spring' migration from the Sabine lands.</blockquote><a href="/state/hirpini">Hirpini</a></li></ul></section>
